WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.221
Standard error and a confidence interval for a pass rate.

2
00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:09.624
If each question scores 0 or 1, the pass rate p-hat is the mean of n Bernoulli variables.

3
00:00:09.904 --> 00:00:20.784
By the central limit theorem it is approximately normal with standard error SE = sqrt(p(1 - p) / n), which you estimate by plugging in p-hat.

4
00:00:21.064 --> 00:00:23.347
For prompt A: sqrt(0.

5
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:25.269
82 * 0.

6
00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:28.152
18 / 200) is about 0.

7
00:00:28.432 --> 00:00:32.208
027, so the 95 percent interval is 0.

8
00:00:32.488 --> 00:00:34.301
82 plus or minus 1.

9
00:00:34.581 --> 00:00:36.160
96 times 0.

10
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.851
027, or roughly 0.

11
00:00:39.131 --> 00:00:41.221
767 to 0.

12
00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:42.995
873.

13
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:50.485
That is the first of Miller’s recommendations: report the standard error of the mean, and an interval, with every eval score.

14
00:00:50.765 --> 00:01:01.283
If scores are fractional (an F1 score, a rubric average), use the sample standard deviation of the per-question scores in place of sqrt(p(1 - p)).

15
00:01:01.563 --> 00:01:05.296
That formula is the Wald interval, and you should know where it breaks.

16
00:01:05.576 --> 00:01:21.149
Brown, Cai, and DasGupta (2001) showed that its actual coverage behaves erratically, falling well below the nominal 95 percent even at sample sizes textbooks call safe, and worst when p is near 0 or 1.

17
00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:23.840
That is exactly where regression suites live.

18
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.000
With 48 of 50 passing, Wald gives 0.

19
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.795
906 to 1.

20
00:01:29.075 --> 00:01:32.488
014, an interval that leaves the unit interval.

21
00:01:32.768 --> 00:01:37.909
With 50 of 50, it gives a width of zero, claiming certainty from 50 questions.

22
00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:42.968
The authors recommend the Wilson interval (or the Jeffreys interval) for small n.

23
00:01:43.248 --> 00:01:50.075
Wilson inverts the score test instead of plugging p-hat into the variance, and for 48 of 50 it gives 0.

24
00:01:50.355 --> 00:01:52.296
865 to 0.

25
00:01:52.576 --> 00:01:55.605
989; for 50 of 50, 0.

26
00:01:55.885 --> 00:01:57.720
929 to 1.

27
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:58.768
0.

28
00:01:59.048 --> 00:02:06.579
The practical rule: use Wilson for any single pass rate, and always for small suites or rates above about 90 percent.

29
00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:18.187
Keep the Wald formula in your head for quick estimates, because its structure (the error shrinks like one over the square root of n) is what drives every sample-size decision later in this lesson.

30
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:21.197
Quadrupling the questions only halves the interval.
